discarded or used), so B3 cannot form 1-2-3. Unlikely useful to others.\n- **Play B7**: Already have B6, B7, B8 in a chow. Another B7? Possibly dangerous if someone is collecting upper dots. But B7 has 3 unshown, 1 used \u2192 2 possibly out, so one left. Risky.\n- **Play J2**: You have two J2s. Discarding one breaks the pair. Since J2 is fully available (4 copies unshown \u2192 none revealed), discarding one is safe unless someone is collecting dragons. But dragon pungs are common.\n- **Play T7**: T7 was just discarded by the previous player, and they have a pung of T7 (via Peng T7), so they have three T7s. They just discarded a fourth? No \u2014 they can't discard after forming a kong unless promoted. Wait: their history shows Peng T7, then later Play T6, etc., but no Gang recorded. So they have an exposed pung of T7. That means all four T7s are likely taken (they have 3, one discarded earlier). Unshown T7: 1 \u2192 possibly still one left. But you have one \u2192 if you discard it, it completes their set? No \u2014 they already have three. So discarding T7 is safe \u2014 they cannot claim it again unless making a kong, but they haven't done so.\n- **Play B6**: Similar to B7. Part of your own chow. B6 unshown: 3 total, one used \u2192 two others possibly out. Safe to discard?\n- **Play J1**: Only two unshown. Previous player discarded J1 twice, next player discarded J1 once. So at least two J1s are out. You have one. Two unshown \u2192 could be one or two left. Not extremely rare, but dragon \u2014 possible target.\n- **Play T3**: Next player has a chow [T1,T2,T3] via Chi \u2014 so they completed that using a discard. You have T3. Could they want another? Possibly for pair or to complete another sequence? But they already used T3 in a chow. Still, danger exists if they are going for All Terminals or something.\n\nNow, consider scoring potential.\n\nCurrent Melds:\n- [T9,T9,T9]: Terminal pung \u2192 1 point for Pung of Terminals/Honors\n- [B6,B7,B8]: Middle chow\n\nHand: T3, T7, B3, B6, B7, J1, J2\u00d72\n\nWait \u2014 J2\u00d72 \u2192 you have a pair of J2 (Green Dragon)\n\nIf you keep J2 pair, and win by self-draw or other, you get:\n- Dragon Pung? No \u2014 only a pair. Unless you draw or claim third.\n- But if you keep J2, you might turn it into a pung.\n\nBut you need to reduce hand to 13 tiles before drawing/discarding. You are at 7 hand tiles + 2 melds (3+3) = 13 physical tiles? Wait: standard hand is 13 tiles before drawing.\n\nWait \u2014 misunderstanding.\n\nStandard: 4 melds (or 3 melds + 1 pair) = 14 tiles.\n\nCurrently, you have:\n- 2 melds \u2192 6 tiles accounted for\n- Hand: 7 tiles \u2192 total 13 tiles \u2192 you are about to draw.\n\nAh! This is critical.\n\nSo you are at the start of your turn. You will draw a tile to make 14, then discard one.\n\nBut the valid actions are all \"Play X\", meaning discard options \u2014 implying you\u2019ve already drawn?\n\nNo \u2014 in Mahjong, \u201cvalid actions\u201d include what you can do *after* drawing.\n\nBut here, the list is only \u201cPlay X\u201d options \u2014 suggesting that you have drawn, and now must discard one of these.\n\nTherefore, you now have 14 tiles in hand (before discard), and these are the possible discards.\n\nBut your hand was listed as 7 tiles: [T3, T7, B3, B6, B7, J1, J2\u00d72] \u2014 that\u2019s 8 tiles.\n\nInconsistency.\n\nCount: T3 (1), T7 (1), B3 (1), B6 (1), B7 (1), J1 (1), J2 (2) \u2192 total 8 tiles.\n\nBut you already have two melds (6 tiles), so total 14 tiles \u2014 yes, you\u2019ve just drawn.\n\nSo you drew one tile to go from 13 to 14, and now must discard one.\n\nThe fact that you have 8 hand tiles means you drew one of them recently.\n\nWhich one did you draw?\n\nCompare with previous state.\n\nBut we don\u2019t have prior hand.\n\nHowever, your last action was \"Play T3\" \u2014 so before that, you had T3, played it.\n\nWait \u2014 game history: ... Play T3, Play B9, Play B1, Play T6, Play T1, Play F3.\n\nLast action was Play T3 \u2014 so you discarded T3.\n\nNow valid actions include Play T3 again? That would mean you drew it back.\n\nPossible.\n\nSimilarly, you played B9, B1, T6, T1, F3 \u2014 all gone.\n\nNow you have: T3, T7, B3, B6, B7, J1, J2\u00d72\n\nSo you must have drawn one of: T3, T7, B3, B6, B7, J1, J2 \u2014 since you just added a tile.\n\nPossibly you drew J2 (making a pair), or T3 (re-acquired), etc.\n\nNow, assess winning possibilities.\n\nCan you win?\n\nTo win, you need a complete hand (4 melds + 1 pair) worth \u22658 points.\n\nCurrent structure:\n- Meld 1: [T9,T9,T9] \u2014 exposed pung (terminal)\n- Meld 2: [B6,B7,B8] \u2014 exposed chow\n- Hand: T3, T7, B3, B6, B7, J1, J2\u00d72\n\nYou have J2\u00d72 \u2192 a pair.\n\nSo if you can form two more melds from the remaining 5 tiles: T3, T7, B3, B6, B7\n\nBut you can only have one more chow or pung.\n\nWait \u2014 you need one more meld (since 2 already, need 4 total).\n\nSo with 5 loose tiles, you need to form one meld from 3 of them, leaving 2 to discard over time \u2014 but you only discard once per turn.\n\nSo you are not yet ready to win.\n\nYou must discard safely while aiming for better shape.\n\nNow, evaluate discard safety and future potential.\n\nOption analysis:\n\n1. **Play B3**:\n   - B3 is terminal. B2 is exhausted (0 unshown), so no one can use B3 in 1-2-3. Could be used in 3-4-5? B4 has 2 unshown, B5 has 2 \u2014 possible. But risky? Maybe not \u2014 B3 not highly sought.\n   - You have no other B3 \u2014 no pair. So no value.\n   - Safe discard.\n\n2. **Play B7**:\n   - You already have B7 in a chow. Another B7? Could be used to make pung? But B7 unshown: 3 total, one used \u2192 2 possibly out. You have one \u2192 so one left. If you discard, maybe someone completes pung.\n   - But previous player has F3, T7 \u2014 not dots. Across has B3,B4,B5 \u2014 lower dots. Next has T1,T2,T3 \u2014 bamboos, but low. So unlikely.\n   - But B7 is in middle range \u2014 possible.\n\n3. **Play B6**:\n   - Similar to B7. Part of your own chow. B6 unshown: 3 total, one used \u2192 2 possibly out. You have one \u2192 one left. Risky?\n\n4. **Play T7**:\n   - Previous player has Peng T7 \u2192 they have three T7s. There are only four T7s. Unshown: 1 \u2192 that must be yours. So if you discard T7, it completes the set \u2014 but they already have three. Can they claim to make a kong? Yes \u2014 if they add the fourth, they can Gang.\n   - But in history, they haven\u2019t done so. But they *can* do it when someone discards T7.\n   - So if you discard T7, they can perform \"Gang\" to take it and form a kong.\n   - That helps them score more and draw a replacement tile \u2014 very dangerous.\n   - **Do NOT discard T7**.\n\n5. **Play J1**:\n   - Dragons are valuable. J1 unshown: 2. You have one. Others have discarded: previous player discarded J1, next player discarded J1 \u2014 so at least two out. You have one, so one or two left.\n   - Someone might be collecting dragons.\n   - You have no pair of J1 \u2014 so no value. Safer than T7.\n\n6. **Play J2**:\n   - You have two J2s. If you discard one, you break the pair.\n   - But keeping the pair gives chance to win with Dragon pair or make pung later.\n   - J2 has 4 unshown \u2014 none revealed yet? But you have two, so 2 unshown. So two left in wall.\n   - High chance to draw or claim.\n   - Better to keep the pair.\n\n7. **Play T3**:\n   - Next player has [T1,T2,T3] chow \u2014 they completed it via Chi, so they used a discard.\n   - They might still want another T3 for pair or pung? Possible.\n   - T3 unshown: 2. You have one. Others: across player discarded T9,T9,T9,T4,T1,T2 \u2014 not many T3s seen. But possible.\n   - Risk: they could claim to make pung if they have two already.\n\nBut do they have two T3s? Their meld is [T1,T2,T3], so they have one T3. To make pung, they\u2019d need two more \u2014 unlikely. So probably safe.\n\nBut safer than T7? Yes.\n\nBest choice: avoid helping opponents.\n\nT7 is most dangerous \u2014 previous player can Gang it.\n\nJ2 pair is valuable \u2014 don\u2019t break unless necessary.\n\nSo best discard is **B3** \u2014 terminal, unused, no pair, and B2 gone so no chow use.\n\nAlternatively, **B6** or **B7** \u2014 but they are part of higher sequences, slightly riskier.\n\n**B3** is safest.\n\nAlso, B3 is a terminal \u2014 if you keep it, it limits your flexibility. Getting rid of terminals early helps move toward All Simples or other patterns.\n\nYou have T9 pung \u2014 already have terminal/honor meld \u2014 so Outside Hand possible, but not strong.\n\nBetter to aim for flexibility.\n\nReason: Discarding B3 removes a terminal tile with little utility (cannot form 1-2-3 due to lack of B2) and poses minimal risk of being claimed, as no opponent appears to be building around B3. In contrast, discarding T7 risks allowing the previous player to form a Kong of T7, which would benefit them significantly. Keeping J2\u00d72 preserves potential for a Dragon pair, which could contribute to scoring.\n\nAnswer: Play B3"}
